<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <section xml:id="sec-section-1-1">
      <title>Section 01 - Introduction to Plane Vectors</title>

<objectives>
  <p><em>Learning Objectives</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Describe a plane vector using correct notation.</li>
    <li>
      Perform the basic vector operations of scalar multiplication and
      vector addition and subtraction.
    </li>
    <li>Determine whether two vectors are equivalent.</li>
  </ul>
</objectives>

      <subsection xml:id="sec-01-01-notation">
        <title>Describe a Plane Vector Using Correct Notation</title>
  <p>
    The term <em>vector</em> is used by scientists to describe a quantity
    (such as displacement, velocity, or force) that has both magnitude
    and direction. A vector is often represented by an arrow, or directed
    line segment. The length of the arrow represents the magnitude of the
    vector, and the arrow points in the direction of the vector.
  </p>

  <p>
    We denote a vector by printing a letter in boldface,
    <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, or by placing an arrow above the letter,
    <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, suppose a particle moves along a line segment from point
    <m>A</m> to point <m>B</m>. The corresponding <em>displacement vector</em>
    <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, shown in <xref ref="figure1"/>,
    has initial point <m>A</m> (the tail) and terminal point <m>B</m>
    (the tip). We indicate this by writing
    <m>\mathbf{v} = \overrightarrow{AB}</m>.
  </p>

<figure xml:id="figure1">
  <image source="images/ch01/Figure1.png">
    <shortdescription>
      Examples of vectors shown with their initial and terminal points.
    </shortdescription>
  </image>
  <caption>
    Examples of vectors shown with their initial and terminal points.
  </caption>
</figure>

<p>
Notice that the vector <m>\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{CD}</m> has the same length and the same direction as
<m>\mathbf{v}</m>, even though it is in a different position.
We say that <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> are <em>equivalent</em> (or <em>equal</em>), and we write
<m>\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{v}</m>.
</p>

<p>
To summarize, <m>\mathbf{AB}=\mathbf{CD}</m> because they have the same length and the same direction.
However, <m>\mathbf{AB}\neq\mathbf{EF}</m> and <m>\mathbf{AB}\neq\mathbf{RQ}</m>
because even though they have the same length, they do not have the same direction.
</p>

<p>
The <term>zero vector</term>, denoted by <m>\mathbf{0}</m>, has length <m>0</m>.
It is the only vector with no specific direction.
</p>

<handout>
  <title>Concept Check</title>

  <checkpoint knowl="no">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose vectors <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> have the same magnitude.
      </p>

      <ol marker="A.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Is that enough to guarantee <m>\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{v}</m>? Why or why not?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> point in the same direction.
            Is that enough to guarantee <m>\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{v}</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            State exactly what conditions must be met for two vectors to be equivalent.
            Do not draw. Explain in words.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </checkpoint>
</handout>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-01-03-basic-ops">
  <title>
    Perform the Basic Vector Operations of Scalar Multiplication and Vector Addition and Subtraction
  </title>

  <p>
    It is possible to multiply a vector by a real number <m>c</m>.
    In this context, we call the real number <m>c</m> a <em>scalar</em> to distinguish it from a vector.
    For instance, we want <m>2\mathbf{v}</m> to be the same as the vector
    <m>\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{v}</m>,
    which has the same direction as <m>\mathbf{v}</m> but is twice as long.
  </p>

<definition knowl="no">
  <title>Scalar Multiplication</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>c</m> is a scalar and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is a vector, then the
      <em>scalar multiple</em> <m>c\mathbf{v}</m> is the vector whose length is
      <m>|c|</m> times the length of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The direction of <m>c\mathbf{v}</m> is the same as the direction of
      <m>\mathbf{v}</m> if <m>c&gt;0</m>, and opposite the direction of
      <m>\mathbf{v}</m> if <m>c&lt;0</m>.
      If <m>c=0</m> or <m>\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}</m>, then
      <m>c\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<p>
In the figure below, notice the following:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>
      Real numbers work like scaling factors, which is why we call them
      <em>scalars</em>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Two nonzero vectors are <em>parallel</em> if they are scalar multiples of one another.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      The vector
    </p>

    <me>
      -\mathbf{v} = (-1)\mathbf{v}
    </me>

    <p>
      has the same length as <m>\mathbf{v}</m> but points in the opposite direction.
      We call this vector the <em>negative</em> of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>

<figure xml:id="figure2">
  <image source="images/ch01/Figure2.png">
    <shortdescription>
      Five arrows show a vector and its scalar multiples, with different lengths in the same direction and in the opposite direction.
    </shortdescription>
  </image>
  <caption>
    A set of vectors showing <m>\mathbf{v}</m> and its scalar multiples:
    <m>2\mathbf{v}, \tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{v}, -\mathbf{v}</m>,
    and <m>-1.5\mathbf{v}</m>.
  </caption>
</figure>
<p>
Suppose a particle moves from point <m>A</m> to point <m>B</m>, so its displacement vector is
<m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>.
The particle then changes direction and moves from <m>B</m> to <m>C</m>, with displacement vector
<m>\overrightarrow{BC}</m>, as shown in the figure below.
</p>
<figure xml:id="figure3">
  <image source="images/ch01/Figure3.png">
    <shortdescription>
A diagram showing vectors from point A to point B and from point A to point C, with an additional vector from point C to point B.
</shortdescription>
  </image>
  <caption>
A diagram showing vectors from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> and from <m>A</m> to <m>C</m>,
with an additional vector from <m>C</m> to <m>B</m>.
</caption>
</figure>
<p>
The combined effect of these displacements is that the particle has moved from <m>A</m> to <m>C</m>.
The resulting displacement vector <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m> is called the
<em>sum</em> of <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{BC}</m>, and we write
</p>

<me>
\overrightarrow{AC}=\overrightarrow{AB}+\overrightarrow{BC}.
</me>

<p>
In general, if we start with vectors <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m>,
we first move <m>\mathbf{v}</m> so that its tail coincides with the tip of <m>\mathbf{u}</m>,
and we define the sum of <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> as follows.
</p>
<definition knowl="no">
  <title>Vector Addition</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> are vectors positioned so that the initial point
      of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is at the terminal point of <m>\mathbf{u}</m>, then the
      <em>sum</em> <m>\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}</m> is the vector from the initial point of
      <m>\mathbf{u}</m> to the terminal point of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>
<figure xml:id="figure4">
  <image source="images/ch01/Figure4.png">
    <shortdescription>
    A vector diagram showing two vectors placed tip to tail and their resulting sum.
    </shortdescription>
  </image>
  <caption>
A vector diagram showing <m>\mathbf{u}</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, and their sum
<m>\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}</m> using the tip-to-tail method.
  </caption>
</figure>
<p>
In the figure below, we start with the same vectors <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m>
and draw another copy of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> with the same initial point as <m>\mathbf{u}</m>.
Completing the parallelogram, we see that
</p>

<me>
\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{u}.
</me>
<figure xml:id="figure5">
  <image source="images/ch01/Figure5.png">
    <shortdescription>
A parallelogram diagram showing two vectors from a common starting point and their combined result.
</shortdescription>
  </image>
<caption>
A parallelogram diagram showing <m>\mathbf{u}</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, and their sum
<m>\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}</m> from a common initial point.
</caption>
</figure>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="sec-01-06-applet">
        <title>Using a Vector Addition Applet</title>
  <handout>
  <title>Concept Check</title>

  <checkpoint knowl="no">
    <statement>
<p>
We will use an interactive applet to visualize vector addition.
</p>

<p>
Suppose the vectors <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> are entered into the applet.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>
      Observe how the vector <m>\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}</m> is formed using the tip-to-tail method.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Reverse the order and observe <m>\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{u}</m>.
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      How does the diagram change?
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Does the resulting vector change?
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Modify the vectors and test several different pairs.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
Based on your observations, explain in words why vector addition is commutative.
</p>

<p>
Do not compute components. Focus on the geometry shown by the applet.
</p>
    </statement>
  </checkpoint>
</handout>

<note knowl="no">
  <title>Vector Addition Applet (Optional Exploration)</title>

  <p>
    You can explore vector addition interactively using the applet below.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><p>Enter your own vectors <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Observe how scaling affects magnitude and direction.</p></li>
    <li><p>Test <m>\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{u}</m> with different vectors.</p></li>
  </ul>

<p>
To explore vector addition interactively, open the
<url href="https://davidmathlogic.com/amit/vectorcommutivity.html">
Vector Addition Commutativity Applet (external link)
</url>.
</p>
</note>

      </subsection>
